  "result": {
    "text": "Certainly! Below is the implementation of the `unique` function that returns sorted, unique elements in a list:\n\n```python\ndef unique(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Return sorted unique elements in a list\n    >>> unique([5, 3, 5, 2, 3, 3, 9, 0, 123])\n    [0, 2, 3, 5, 9, 123]\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_elements = sorted(set(l))\n    return unique_elements\n\n# Example usage\nif __name__ == \"____main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()\n```\n\nThis function works by first converting the list to a set to remove duplicates, then converting the set to a list, and finally converting it back to a list. The `sorted` function is used to sort the unique elements.",
